{#- Presentation only. Every number is m(key, fmt) (a data-metric span from reports/metrics.json); v(key) is used
    ONLY in conditions. tests/test_report.py lints the rendered text for digits outside spans. -#}
{%- set H = "splits.heldout.policies" -%}
{%- set S = "splits.shifted.policies" -%}
{%- set D = "splits.dev.policies" -%}
{%- set FS = "splits.shifted.analysis" -%}
{%- macro okbad(flag, yes="meets", no="breach") -%}
{%- if flag -%}<span class="pass">✓ {{ yes }}</span>{%- else -%}<span class="fail">✗ {{ no }}</span>{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
{%- macro sw(k) -%}<span class="swatch" style="background:{{ T.POLICY[k].color }}"></span>{%- endmacro -%}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Less Compute Report</title>
<meta name="description" content="Efficiency layer for a fictional bilingual claims desk: cost per successful task on held-out, with CIs.">
<style>{{ css }}</style>
<script>{{ plotlyjs }}</script>
</head>
<body>
<header class="top">
  <span>HUMAIN × Misk Hackathon · Challenge: Deliver AI with Less Compute</span>
  <span class="fiction">FICTIONAL insurer and rules · synthetic data · not Najm, SAMA or any real insurer</span>
</header>
<main>

<!-- 1. HERO -->
<section class="hero">
  <div class="kicker">Held-out result · {{ m("splits.heldout.n", "int") }} sealed tickets, evaluated once</div>
  <h1>Pick the cheapest model and effort that still meets each task's accuracy floor.</h1>
  <div class="ar">طبقة كفاءة تختار أرخص نموذج ومستوى جهد يحقق الدقة المطلوبة لكل مهمة، دون انتظار غير ضروري.</div>
  <div class="hero-grid">
    <div class="hero-figure">{{ m(H ~ ".O1_live.saving_nocache", "pct1") }}
      <small>lower cost per successful task than always-best (B1) · O1 {{ T.LIVE }}, held-out ·
        {{ T.HEADLINE }}. 95% CI {{ ci(H ~ ".O1_live.saving_nocache", "pct1") }}.
        CPST {{ m(H ~ ".B1.cpst_nocache", "usd5") }} → {{ m(H ~ ".O1_live.cpst_nocache", "usd5") }}.
        {{ T.CACHED }}: {{ m(H ~ ".O1_live.saving_cached", "pct1") }} lower {{ ci(H ~ ".O1_live.saving_cached", "pct1") }}.</small>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="lede">Most tickets don't need the biggest model. O1 answers them with Haiku, has a second Haiku check
        the hard ones, and calls Opus only when the checks fail.</p>
      <div class="badges">
        {%- set all_ok = v(H ~ ".O1_live.meets_floors") %}
        <span class="badge {{ 'ok' if all_ok else 'bad' }}">{{ '✓' if all_ok else '✗' }} accuracy floors: {{ 'all tasks met' if all_ok else 'breach' }}</span>
        <span class="badge">accuracy {{ m(H ~ ".O1_live.acc", "f3") }} (B1 {{ m(H ~ ".B1.acc", "f3") }})</span>
        <span class="badge">p95 {{ m(H ~ ".O1_live.p95_s", "s2") }} {{ T.LIVE }}</span>
        <span class="badge">success {{ m(H ~ ".O1_live.success", "f3") }} (B1 {{ m(H ~ ".B1.success", "f3") }})</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="shifted-line"><b>Under shifted traffic</b> (unseen phrasings, dialect, policy change, adversarial;
    {{ T.SIMULATED }}): O1 still costs {{ m(S ~ ".O1.saving_nocache", "pct1") }} less per successful task than B1
    {{ ci(S ~ ".O1.saving_nocache", "pct1") }}, but its accuracy drops to {{ m(S ~ ".O1.acc", "f3") }} vs B1's
    {{ m(S ~ ".B1.acc", "f3") }} and it {{ 'meets every floor' if v(S ~ ".O1.meets_floors") else 'breaches two task floors' }}.
    The fail-safe analysis (drift alarm → revert the alarmed task to B1's config) recovers accuracy to
    {{ m(FS ~ ".failsafe.acc", "f3") }} at CPST {{ m(FS ~ ".failsafe.cpst_nocache", "usd5") }}
    (B1 {{ m(S ~ ".B1.cpst_nocache", "usd5") }}). <a href="#robustness">Details</a>.</div>
</section>

<!-- 2. PROBLEM -->
<section>
  <div class="kicker">The problem</div>
  <h2>Always using the best model pays premium prices for easy tickets</h2>
  <p class="lede">On dev, the cheapest config ({{ m("o1_config.first", "raw") }}) answers {{ m("problem.cheap.acc", "pct1") }}
    of tickets correctly; the most accurate ({{ m("o1_config.b1", "raw") }}) answers {{ m("problem.best.acc", "pct1") }} and costs
    {{ m("problem.cost_ratio", "x1") }} as much per call ({{ T.HEADLINE }}). On held-out, a Haiku config is the cheapest
    correct answer within budget for {{ m("splits.heldout.haiku_suffices", "pct1") }} of tickets. The skill is spotting
    the few tickets that need more, cheaply and without waiting.</p>
  <div class="receipts">
    {%- for pol in ("B1", "O1") %}
    {%- set r = "receipt." ~ pol %}
    <div class="receipt {{ 'o1' if pol == 'O1' else '' }}">
      <h3>{{ sw(pol) }}{{ T.POLICY[pol].label }} · dev ticket {{ m("receipt.ticket_id", "raw") }} ({{ m("receipt.task", "raw") }}, {{ m("receipt.language", "raw") }})</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table>
        <tr><th>call</th><th>config</th><th class="r">tokens in / out*</th><th class="r">{{ T.HEADLINE }}</th><th class="r">{{ T.CACHED }}</th><th class="r">latency</th><th>checks</th></tr>
        {%- for ln in v(r ~ ".lines") %}{% set k = r ~ ".lines." ~ loop.index0 %}
        <tr><td>{{ m(k ~ ".role", "raw") }}</td><td>{{ m(k ~ ".config", "raw") }}</td>
          <td class="r">{{ m(k ~ ".input_tokens", "int") }} / {{ m(k ~ ".output_tokens", "int") }}</td>
          <td class="r">{{ m(k ~ ".usd_nocache", "usd5") }}</td><td class="r">{{ m(k ~ ".usd_cached", "usd5") }}</td>
          <td class="r">{{ m(k ~ ".latency_s", "s2") }}</td><td>{{ okbad(ln.checks_pass, "pass", "fail") }}</td></tr>
        {%- endfor %}
        <tr class="total"><td>total</td><td></td><td></td><td class="r">{{ m(r ~ ".usd_nocache", "usd5") }}</td>
          <td class="r">{{ m(r ~ ".usd_cached", "usd5") }}</td><td class="r">{{ m(r ~ ".latency_s", "s2") }}</td>
          <td>{{ okbad(v(r ~ ".correct") and v(r ~ ".success"), "correct, in budget", "failed") }}</td></tr>
      </table></div>
    </div>
    {%- endfor %}
  </div>
  <figcaption>{{ caption("receipt") }}. Ticket chosen by a declared rule (first Arabic dev adjudicate ticket the Haiku pair settles; <code>report/collect.py: demo_set</code>). Latency budget {{ m("receipt.budget_s", "s1") }}. The two Haiku calls run in parallel, so O1's latency is the slower of the two. *Each model counts tokens with its own tokenizer; don't compare token counts across models.</figcaption>

<div class="card"><figure><h3>{{ title("cost_vs_accuracy") }}</h3>{{ figs["cost_vs_accuracy"] }}
  <figcaption>{{ caption("cost_vs_accuracy") }}. Grey points = the nine configs (each answering every dev ticket); step line = Pareto frontier; coloured markers = policies (O1 {{ T.SIMULATED }} by replay). Log x-axis.</figcaption></figure></div>
</section>

<!-- 3. HOW IT WORKS -->
<section>
  <div class="kicker">How it works</div>
  <h2>A cost ladder with checks between the rungs</h2>
  <p class="lede">For each task, configs, floors and budgets were chosen on dev and frozen before anything was scored.
    faq, intent and extract go to the cheapest config that meets their floor on dev
    ({{ m("o1_config.faq", "raw") }}, {{ m("o1_config.intent", "raw") }}, {{ m("o1_config.extract", "raw") }}); no checks.
    Adjudicate (a payout decision) climbs the ladder:</p>
  <div class="ladder" role="img" aria-label="Adjudicate ladder: two Haiku configs in parallel, checks, then Opus">
    <div class="rung o1"><div class="cfg">{{ m("o1_config.first", "raw") }} ‖ {{ m("o1_config.second", "raw") }}</div>
      <div class="sub">in parallel · {{ m("splits.heldout.sankey.rung_cost_nocache.haiku-low", "usd4") }} + {{ m("splits.heldout.sankey.rung_cost_nocache.haiku-medium", "usd4") }} per call ({{ T.LIVE }}, {{ T.HEADLINE }})</div></div>
    <div class="arrow">→</div>
    <div class="gate"><b>checks</b>V0 valid JSON · V1 grounded in the ticket · V2 payout recomputes · V3 both Haikus agree
      (wait at most {{ m("o1_config.deadline_s", "s1") }} for the second)</div>
    <div class="arrow">→</div>
    <div class="rung top"><div class="cfg">{{ m("o1_config.top", "raw") }}</div>
      <div class="sub">only if a check fails · {{ m("splits.heldout.sankey.rung_cost_nocache.opus-low", "usd4") }} per call; starts the moment the first answer fails</div></div>
  </div>
  <p class="muted" style="font-size:14px">Dropped along the way: Tier 0 (rule-based short-cuts; answered nothing), two learned routers (see
    <a href="#negatives">What didn't work</a>), and higher effort levels (next chart).</p>
  <div class="card"><figure><h3>{{ title("ablations") }}</h3>{{ figs["ablations"] }}
    <figcaption>{{ caption("ablations") }}. Each row switches one O1 layer off on dev. Dotted line = the pre-registered adjudicate floor ({{ m("ablation_ref.needed", "int") }} of {{ m("ablation_ref.n", "int") }} correct). Labels: CPST change vs O1 ({{ T.HEADLINE }}), paired 95% CI.</figcaption></figure></div>
  <div class="card"><figure><h3>{{ title("effort") }}</h3>{{ figs["effort"] }}
    <figcaption>{{ caption("effort") }}. Bars start at zero; labels: cost vs low effort, accuracy change vs low with a paired 95% CI.</figcaption></figure></div>
</section>

<!-- 4. PROOF -->
<section>
  <div class="kicker">The proof</div>
  <h2>Held-out: every policy, the oracle, with 95% CIs</h2>
  <p class="lede">CPST = all USD spent (every call, second opinion, escalation) ÷ tickets answered correctly <em>and</em>
    within the task's latency budget. Baselines, O1 {{ T.SIMULATED }} and the oracle replay the held-out matrix
    ({{ prov(v("provenance.runs.heldout_matrix")) }}); O1 {{ T.LIVE }} is the real run
    ({{ prov(v("provenance.runs.heldout_live")) }}). Savings are vs B1, paired bootstrap.</p>
  <div class="card table-wrap"><table>
    <tr><th>policy</th><th class="r">accuracy</th><th class="r">success</th><th class="r">p95</th>
      <th class="r">CPST, {{ T.HEADLINE }}</th><th class="r">saving vs B1</th><th class="r">CPST, {{ T.CACHED }}</th><th class="r">saving vs B1</th><th>floors</th></tr>
    {%- for k, name in (("B1", "B1 always-best (" ~ v("o1_config.b1") ~ ")"), ("B2", "B2 always-cheapest"), ("B3", "B3 best static config per task"),
        ("B4", "B4 cheapest single config meeting every dev floor"), ("B5", "B5 naive cascade"), ("O1", "O1 (" ~ T.SIMULATED ~ ")"),
        ("O1_live", "O1 (" ~ T.LIVE ~ ")"), ("Oracle", "Oracle: cheapest correct config within budget")) %}
    {%- set p = H ~ "." ~ k %}
    <tr class="{{ 'o1' if k.startswith('O1') else ('oracle' if k == 'Oracle' else '') }}">
      <td>{{ sw(k) }}{{ name }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".acc", "f3") }} {{ ci(p ~ ".acc", "f3") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".success", "f3") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".p95_s", "s2") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".cpst_nocache", "usd5") }} {{ ci(p ~ ".cpst_nocache", "usd5") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{% if k != "B1" %}{{ m(p ~ ".saving_nocache", "pct1") }} {{ ci(p ~ ".saving_nocache", "pct1") }}{% else %}reference{% endif %}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".cpst_cached", "usd5") }} {{ ci(p ~ ".cpst_cached", "usd5") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{% if k != "B1" %}{{ m(p ~ ".saving_cached", "pct1") }} {{ ci(p ~ ".saving_cached", "pct1") }}{% else %}reference{% endif %}</td>
      <td>{{ okbad(v(p ~ ".meets_floors"), "all met", "breach") }}</td></tr>
    {%- endfor %}
  </table>
  <p style="font-size:14.5px"><b>At scale:</b> per {{ m("splits.heldout.per_100k_n", "int") }} tickets like these, O1 {{ T.LIVE }} would spend
    {{ m("splits.heldout.per_100k.O1_live.nocache", "usd2") }} ({{ T.HEADLINE }}) / {{ m("splits.heldout.per_100k.O1_live.cached", "usd2") }} ({{ T.CACHED }})
    vs always-best {{ m("splits.heldout.per_100k.B1.nocache", "usd2") }} / {{ m("splits.heldout.per_100k.B1.cached", "usd2") }},
    from the held-out mean cost per ticket.</p>
  <p class="muted" style="font-size:13.5px">Oracle gap captured, (CPST<sub>B1</sub> − CPST<sub>O1</sub>) ÷ (CPST<sub>B1</sub> − CPST<sub>oracle</sub>):
    O1 {{ T.SIMULATED }} {{ m("splits.heldout.oracle_gap.nocache", "pct1") }} ({{ T.HEADLINE }}),
    {{ m("splits.heldout.oracle_gap.cached", "pct1") }} ({{ T.CACHED }}); O1 {{ T.LIVE }}
    {{ m("splits.heldout.oracle_gap_live.nocache", "pct1") }} / {{ m("splits.heldout.oracle_gap_live.cached", "pct1") }}
    (point values; no CI computed).</p>
  {%- set b2_ok, b2_cheaper = v(H ~ ".B2.meets_floors"), v(H ~ ".B2.cpst_nocache") < v(H ~ ".O1_live.cpst_nocache") %}
  {%- set over = [] %}{% for t in tasks %}{% if v(H ~ ".O1_live.per_task." ~ t ~ ".p95_s") > v("floors." ~ t ~ ".budget_s") %}{% set _ = over.append(t) %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
  <div class="note"><b>Two things to read with this table.</b>
    <p style="margin:6px 0 0">B2 (always-cheapest) {{ 'met every floor' if b2_ok else 'breached a floor' }} on held-out and was
      {{ 'cheaper' if b2_cheaper else 'more expensive' }} than O1 ({{ m(H ~ ".B2.cpst_nocache", "usd5") }} vs O1 {{ T.LIVE }} {{ m(H ~ ".O1_live.cpst_nocache", "usd5") }}),
      but it {{ 'breached' if not v(D ~ ".B2.meets_floors") else 'met' }} the floors on dev (accuracy {{ m(D ~ ".B2.acc", "f3") }})
      and {{ 'breached' if not v(S ~ ".B2.meets_floors") else 'met' }} them on shifted (accuracy {{ m(S ~ ".B2.acc", "f3") }}).
      O1 was chosen on dev, where B2 was not an option.</p>
    <p style="margin:6px 0 0">{{ T.LIVE | capitalize }} p95 latency {{ 'exceeded' if v(H ~ ".O1_live.p95_s") > v(H ~ ".O1.p95_s") else 'stayed within' }}
      the {{ T.SIMULATED }} value ({{ m(H ~ ".O1_live.p95_s", "s2") }} vs {{ m(H ~ ".O1.p95_s", "s2") }}){% if over %};{% for t in over %} {{ t }} ran over its latency budget {{ T.LIVE }} (p95 {{ m(H ~ ".O1_live.per_task." ~ t ~ ".p95_s", "s2") }} vs budget {{ m("floors." ~ t ~ ".budget_s", "s1") }}){{ "," if not loop.last }}{% endfor %},
      so {{ T.LIVE }} success is {{ m(H ~ ".O1_live.success", "f3") }} vs {{ m(H ~ ".O1.success", "f3") }} {{ T.SIMULATED }}{% endif %}.</p>
  </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card"><figure><h3>{{ title("cpst_forest") }}</h3>{{ figs["cpst_forest"] }}
    <figcaption>{{ caption("cpst_forest") }}. Dot = CPST, whisker = 95% CI (bootstrap); log scale. An open marker with ✗ = breaches a per-task accuracy floor.
      B4 and B5 are in the table only: their choices coincide with B1 ({{ m("o1_config.b1", "raw") }}) and with B2 when {{ m("o1_config.first", "raw") }} passes V0.</figcaption></figure></div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">O1 {{ T.LIVE }} on held-out, per task and per language</h3>
  <div class="card table-wrap"><table>
    <tr><th>subset</th><th class="r">n</th><th class="r">accuracy</th><th class="r">floor</th><th class="r">success</th>
      <th class="r">CPST, {{ T.HEADLINE }}</th><th class="r">saving vs B1</th><th class="r">CPST, {{ T.CACHED }}</th><th class="r">saving vs B1</th></tr>
    {%- for g in ("task_faq", "task_intent", "task_extract", "task_adjudicate", "lang_en", "lang_ar") %}
    {%- set p = H ~ ".O1_live.breakdown." ~ g %}
    <tr><td>{{ g.replace("task_", "").replace("lang_", "language ") }}</td><td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".n", "int") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".acc", "f3") }} {{ ci(p ~ ".acc", "f3") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{% if g.startswith("task_") %}{{ m("floors." ~ g[5:] ~ ".floor", "f3") }}{% endif %}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".success", "f3") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".cpst_nocache", "usd5") }} {{ ci(p ~ ".cpst_nocache", "usd5") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".saving_nocache", "pct1") }} {{ ci(p ~ ".saving_nocache", "pct1") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".cpst_cached", "usd5") }} {{ ci(p ~ ".cpst_cached", "usd5") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".saving_cached", "pct1") }} {{ ci(p ~ ".saving_cached", "pct1") }}</td></tr>
    {%- endfor %}
  </table></div>

  <div class="card"><figure><h3>{{ title("sim_vs_live") }}</h3>{{ figs["sim_vs_live"] }}
    <figcaption>{{ caption("sim_vs_live") }}. {{ T.SIMULATED }} = replay of recorded calls with modelled parallel timing; {{ T.LIVE }} = real parallel calls, wall-clock latency.
      Same correctness on {{ m("splits.heldout.sim_vs_live.same_correct", "int") }} of {{ m("splits.heldout.sim_vs_live.n", "int") }} tickets, same path on {{ m("splits.heldout.sim_vs_live.same_path", "int") }}.
      {{ T.LIVE }} CPST {{ m(H ~ ".O1_live.cpst_nocache", "usd5") }} vs {{ T.SIMULATED }} {{ m(H ~ ".O1.cpst_nocache", "usd5") }} ({{ T.HEADLINE }}).</figcaption></figure></div>
</section>

<!-- 5. WHERE THE MONEY GOES -->
<section>
  <div class="kicker">Where the money goes</div>
  <h2>Adjudicate carries most of the bill</h2>
  <div class="card"><figure><h3>{{ title("spend_by_task") }}</h3>{{ figs["spend_by_task"] }}
    <figcaption>{{ caption("spend_by_task") }}. Bars start at zero. {{ T.CACHED }}: O1 {{ T.LIVE }} {{ m("splits.heldout.spend_by_task.total.O1_live.cached", "usd2") }} vs B1 {{ m("splits.heldout.spend_by_task.total.B1.cached", "usd2") }}.</figcaption></figure></div>
</section>

<!-- 6. INSIDE ONE REQUEST -->
<section>
  <div class="kicker">Inside one request</div>
  <h2>Where each adjudicate ticket ended up, {{ T.LIVE }} on held-out</h2>
  <div class="card"><figure><h3>{{ title("adjudicate_sankey") }}</h3>{{ figs["adjudicate_sankey"] }}
    <figcaption>{{ caption("adjudicate_sankey") }}. Band width = ticket count. Node costs = mean cost per call in the {{ T.LIVE }} run ({{ T.HEADLINE }}). Latency budget {{ m("splits.heldout.sankey.budget_s", "s1") }}.</figcaption></figure></div>
</section>

<!-- 7. ROBUSTNESS -->
<section id="robustness">
  <div class="kicker">Robustness</div>
  <h2>Shifted traffic breaks two floors; a cheap audit catches it</h2>
  <div class="card"><figure><h3>{{ title("robustness") }}</h3>{{ figs["robustness"] }}
    <figcaption>{{ caption("robustness") }}. Dotted line = frozen per-task floor; ✓/✗ = met/breached. Open diamond = best single config on shifted, picked on shifted for diagnosis only (no policy uses it): it shows the adjudicate floor is out of reach for every config. Fail-safe: <b>analysis added after observing shifted; it selects nothing from shifted; the held-out run uses the dev-frozen O1.</b></figcaption></figure></div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">Drift monitor: how big must a labelled audit sample be?</h3>
  <p>Audit sizes below are the <b>total</b> audit sample (all tasks together, stratified by task); the faq and adjudicate
    columns give how many of those tickets fall in each task. On shifted, a firing is a correct detection; on dev
    (where O1 meets every floor) it is a false alarm. Each row is the share of resampled audits in which the detector fired.</p>
  <div class="card table-wrap"><table>
    <tr><th>detector</th><th class="r">audit size (total)</th><th class="r">faq / adjudicate in it</th><th class="r">detects shifted drift</th><th class="r">false alarms on dev</th></tr>
    {%- for det in ("binomial", "naive") %}
    {%- for i in range(v("monitor") | length) %}{% set row = v("monitor")[i] %}
    {%- if row.split == "shifted" and row.detector == det %}
    {%- set j = namespace(idx=0) %}{% for k in range(v("monitor") | length) %}{% if v("monitor")[k].split == "dev" and v("monitor")[k].detector == det and v("monitor")[k].n == row.n %}{% set j.idx = k %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
    <tr><td>{{ det }}{{ " (exact test)" if det == "binomial" else " (below floor)" }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m("monitor." ~ i ~ ".n", "int") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m("monitor." ~ i ~ ".faq_n", "int") }} / {{ m("monitor." ~ i ~ ".adjudicate_n", "int") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m("monitor." ~ i ~ ".either", "pct1") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m("monitor." ~ j.idx ~ ".either", "pct1") }}</td></tr>
    {%- endif %}{%- endfor %}{%- endfor %}
  </table>
  {%- set mon = namespace(i=0) %}{% for i in range(v("monitor") | length) %}{% if v("monitor")[i].split == "shifted" and v("monitor")[i].detector == "binomial" and v("monitor")[i].n == 100 %}{% set mon.i = i %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
  <p class="muted" style="font-size:13.5px">Detection power depends on the per-task counts, not the total: in a binomial audit of
    {{ m("monitor." ~ mon.i ~ ".n", "int") }} tickets ({{ m("monitor." ~ mon.i ~ ".faq_n", "int") }} faq, {{ m("monitor." ~ mon.i ~ ".adjudicate_n", "int") }} adjudicate),
    the faq alarm fires in {{ m("monitor." ~ mon.i ~ ".faq", "pct1") }} of shifted samples and the adjudicate alarm in {{ m("monitor." ~ mon.i ~ ".adjudicate", "pct1") }}.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:28px">Policy change and adversarial tickets (shifted split, frozen O1, {{ T.SIMULATED }})</h3>
  <p>Policy change: some tickets were generated under a second rules file; the system was pointed at the new policy document,
    with no code or prompt change. O1 answered {{ m("splits.shifted.scenario.policy_change.correct", "int") }} of
    {{ m("splits.shifted.scenario.policy_change.n", "int") }} correctly ({{ m("splits.shifted.scenario.policy_change.acc", "f3") }}),
    below the floors. Adversarial (prompt injection, garbled, off-topic, rambling):
    {{ m("splits.shifted.scenario.adversarial.correct", "int") }} of {{ m("splits.shifted.scenario.adversarial.n", "int") }}
    ({{ m("splits.shifted.scenario.adversarial.acc", "f3") }}). Normal shifted tickets:
    {{ m("splits.shifted.scenario.normal.acc", "f3") }}.</p>
</section>

<!-- 8. WHAT DIDN'T WORK -->
<section id="negatives">
  <div class="kicker" style="color:var(--bad)">What didn't work</div>
  <h2>Negative results, reported at full weight</h2>
  <div class="neg-grid">
    <div class="neg"><h3>Both routers were dropped</h3>
      <p>A local classifier (ROC AUC {{ m("routers.local_auc", "f3") }}) and the Jev decision model (AUC {{ m("routers.jev_auc", "f3") }})
        could not predict which adjudicate tickets need Opus. Even a perfect router would save only
        {{ m("routers.ceiling_cpst_gain", "pct1") }}, below the pre-registered bar of {{ m("routers.bar_cpst", "pct0") }}.</p>
      <div class="src">{{ prov(v("routers.source")) }} (parsed)</div></div>
    <div class="neg"><h3>Always-cheapest beat O1 on held-out</h3>
      <p>On held-out, B2 meets every floor at CPST {{ m(H ~ ".B2.cpst_nocache", "usd5") }} vs O1 {{ T.LIVE }}
        {{ m(H ~ ".O1_live.cpst_nocache", "usd5") }}. O1's adjudicate checks were needed on dev, where B2 reaches only
        {{ m(D ~ ".B2.acc", "f3") }} accuracy, but not on this sample.</p></div>
    <div class="neg"><h3>Shifted traffic breaches two floors</h3>
      <p>Frozen O1 on shifted: faq {{ m(S ~ ".O1.per_task.faq.acc", "f3") }} vs floor {{ m("floors.faq.floor", "f3") }};
        adjudicate {{ m(S ~ ".O1.per_task.adjudicate.acc", "f3") }} vs {{ m("floors.adjudicate.floor", "f3") }}. No single config reaches
        the adjudicate floor on shifted (best {{ m("splits.shifted.best_single_adjudicate_acc", "f3") }}).</p></div>
    <div class="neg"><h3>Correlated verifier errors</h3>
      <p>The checks can't catch two Haikus that pass V0–V2 and agree on the same wrong answer. Of
        {{ m("splits.shifted.verifier.first_wrong", "int") }} wrong first answers on shifted, O1 still accepted
        {{ m("splits.shifted.verifier.accepted_wrong", "int") }}; {{ m("splits.shifted.verifier.agreed_wrong", "int") }} with both
        Haikus agreeing (the rest: second opinion late). Dev: {{ m("splits.dev.verifier.agreed_wrong", "int") }} of
        {{ m("splits.dev.verifier.first_wrong", "int") }}; held-out {{ T.SIMULATED }}: {{ m("splits.heldout.verifier.agreed_wrong", "int") }} of
        {{ m("splits.heldout.verifier.first_wrong", "int") }}.</p></div>
    <div class="neg"><h3>Re-picking configs on the audit sample made it worse</h3>
      <p>Re-running the selection rule on an audit fold: shifted adjudicate accuracy {{ m(FS ~ ".frozen.per_task.adjudicate.acc", "f3") }}
        → {{ m(FS ~ ".recalibrated.per_task.adjudicate.acc", "f3") }}, overall CPST {{ m(FS ~ ".frozen.cpst_nocache", "usd5") }}
        → {{ m(FS ~ ".recalibrated.cpst_nocache", "usd5") }}. Detection works; selection on a small sample is too noisy.</p></div>
    <div class="neg"><h3>Simulated latency was optimistic</h3>
      <p>Parallel timing was modelled from separate calls. {{ T.LIVE }} p95 was {{ m(H ~ ".O1_live.p95_s", "s2") }} vs
        {{ m(H ~ ".O1.p95_s", "s2") }} {{ T.SIMULATED }}; {{ T.LIVE }} faq success {{ m(H ~ ".O1_live.per_task.faq.success", "f3") }}
        vs {{ m(H ~ ".O1.per_task.faq.success", "f3") }} (budget {{ m("floors.faq.budget_s", "s1") }}).</p></div>
    <div class="neg"><h3>Name collisions in the synthetic tickets</h3>
      <p>The generator sometimes gives two people the same first name. Dev has {{ m("names.dev_visible", "int") }} visible clashes
        (shifted {{ m("names.shifted_visible", "int") }}); on them Opus scores {{ m("names.opus_clash_acc", "f3") }} vs
        {{ m("names.opus_other_acc", "f3") }} elsewhere. Data left unchanged.</p>
      <div class="src">{{ prov(v("names.source")) }} (parsed)</div></div>
    <div class="neg"><h3>Suspect shifted tickets</h3>
      <p>{{ m("splits.shifted.opus_high_adjudicate_wrong", "int") }} shifted adjudicate tickets defeat even opus-high; read by hand, they look like
        generator defects (typos and contradictions on the deciding fact). They stay in the data; excluding them would not remove the breach.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 9. SCENARIOS -->
<section>
  <div class="kicker">Scenarios</div>
  <h2>What if prices change, or a model goes down?</h2>
  <p>Replay only, no API calls. The controller re-applies the frozen selection rules on dev to the changed world; the
    scored split is never used to choose.</p>
  <div class="card table-wrap"><table>
    <tr><th>split</th><th>scenario</th><th>controller's picks</th><th class="r">O1 CPST, {{ T.HEADLINE }}</th><th class="r">B1 CPST</th><th class="r">saving vs B1</th><th class="r">accuracy</th><th>floors</th></tr>
    {%- for sp in ("dev", "shifted") %}{% for i in range(v("scenarios." ~ sp) | length) %}{% set p = "scenarios." ~ sp ~ "." ~ i %}
    <tr><td>{{ sp }}</td><td>{{ m(p ~ ".name", "raw") }}</td>
      <td style="font-size:12.5px">faq {{ m(p ~ ".picks.faq", "raw") }}, intent {{ m(p ~ ".picks.intent", "raw") }}, extract {{ m(p ~ ".picks.extract", "raw") }}, adjudicate {{ 'Haiku pair → ' if v(p ~ '.picks.adjudicate_cascade') else '' }}{{ m(p ~ ".picks.adjudicate_top", "raw") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".cpst_nocache", "usd5") }}</td><td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".b1_cpst_nocache", "usd5") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".saving_nocache", "pct1") }} {{ ci(p ~ ".saving_nocache", "pct1") }}</td>
      <td class="r">{{ m(p ~ ".acc", "f3") }}</td><td>{{ okbad(v(p ~ ".meets_floors"), "met", "breach") }}</td></tr>
    {%- endfor %}{% endfor %}
  </table></div>
</section>

<!-- 10. METHOD -->
<section>
  <div class="kicker">Method, caveats, provenance</div>
  <h2>How these numbers were made</h2>
  <ul class="caveats">
    <li>Data: fully synthetic, generated from one rules file that is the source of both the policy document and the gold labels. English and Arabic (MSA and Saudi dialect).
      <b>The insurer and its rules are fictional.</b></li>
    <li>Configs: three Claude models × three effort levels. Floors (best dev accuracy − tolerance) and latency budgets were frozen on dev before any optimisation (tag <code>floors-v1</code>).</li>
    <li>Evaluation: counterfactual replay over a recorded matrix of every config on every ticket. Held-out was sealed, then evaluated once at <code>{{ v("provenance.heldout_tag") }}</code> with O1 run {{ T.LIVE }}.</li>
    <li>CIs: paired bootstrap over tickets, {{ m("provenance.bootstrap.resamples", "int") }} resamples, fixed seed.</li>
    <li>“{{ T.HEADLINE }}” prices all prompt tokens at the base input rate; “{{ T.CACHED }}” is what was billed.</li>
    <li>Parallel timing in replay is modelled; held-out O1 was also measured {{ T.LIVE }} (gap shown above).</li>
    <li>Oracle: per ticket, the cheapest config that is correct within budget; a ticket no config solves is charged the cheapest config and counts as a failure.</li>
    <li>Router thresholds were tuned in-sample on dev, which favours the routers; their negative result is conservative.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="card" style="font-size:13.5px">
    <div>Runs: {{ prov(v("provenance.runs.dev")) }} · {{ prov(v("provenance.runs.shifted")) }} · {{ prov(v("provenance.runs.heldout_matrix")) }} · {{ prov(v("provenance.runs.heldout_live")) }} · {{ prov(v("provenance.runs.heldout_export")) }}</div>
    <div>Config hash: dev {{ prov(v("provenance.config_hash.dev")) }} · shifted {{ prov(v("provenance.config_hash.shifted")) }} · held-out {{ prov(v("provenance.config_hash.heldout")) }}</div>
    <div>Report built at git {{ prov(v("provenance.build_git_sha")) }}; held-out run at {{ prov(v("provenance.heldout_tag")) }} {{ prov(v("provenance.heldout_git_sha")) }}</div>
    <div class="muted">Every number on this page is a <code>data-metric</code> span read from <code>reports/metrics.json</code>; decisions are logged in <code>docs/DECISIONS.md</code>.</div>
  </div>
</section>
</main>
<footer>Fictional demo insurer and rules; synthetic data; not real Najm, SAMA or insurer rules. Self-contained page: open from disk, no network needed.</footer>
